Package: nexus
Section: contrib/metapackages
Architecture: all
Depends: nexus-board (= ${source:Version}),
         nexus-captive-portal (= ${source:Version}),
         nexus-web-panel (= ${source:Version}),
         nexus-web-terminal (= ${source:Version}),
         nexus-archive-keyring,
         ${misc:Depends}
Description: Tridex Nexus board software (metapackage)
 Installs everything the Nexus PCB runs: the Wi-Fi setup hotspot with its
 captive portal, the web panel and the browser terminal, and the signing key
 of the Nexus apt repository, so the board keeps receiving updates through
 apt.

Package: nexus-captive-portal
Section: net
Architecture: all
# preinst diverts comitup's conffile /etc/comitup.conf; that only works once
# comitup is fully configured, which plain Depends does not guarantee.
Pre-Depends: comitup
Depends: network-manager,
         dbus,
         passwd,
         python3,
         python3-dbus,
         python3-flask,
         python3-libgpiod (>= 2),
         ${misc:Depends}
Description: Wi-Fi setup hotspot and captive portal for the Tridex Nexus PCB
 When the board cannot join a known network it opens the hotspot
 "Nexus-<nnn>" with a captive portal that lists nearby networks and takes
 the password. On a fresh board the portal first asks for the password of
 the admin account "tridex", which ships locked. Built on comitup; this
 package provides its configuration and the Nexus portal, which runs as an
 unprivileged user and sets the password through a root helper that can
 set nothing else.
 .
 Also drives the Wi-Fi LED (blinking: setup needed, on: connected) and the
 Wi-Fi reset button: held for 10 s it locks the admin account again,
 deletes its SSH keys and the saved Wi-Fi networks, and reboots into setup.
 .
 Replaces /etc/comitup.conf (via dpkg-divert) and hands wlan0 from ifupdown
 to NetworkManager.

Package: nexus-board
Section: misc
Architecture: all
Depends: python3,
         python3-libgpiod (>= 2),
         ${misc:Depends}
Description: LED ring and camera port LEDs of the Tridex Nexus PCB
 nexus-board.service holds the board's LED GPIOs: the LED ring keeps its
 last state across reboots (a new board starts with it on), and each
 camera port's LED is lit while a camera is plugged into that port.
 "nexus-board" shows the state and switches the ring; the Nexus web panel
 does the same through the service's root-only socket.

Package: nexus-web-panel
# contrib: free itself, but runs Autodarts, which is not free software.
Section: contrib/net
Architecture: all
Depends: python3,
         libcrypt1,
         ${misc:Depends}
# The panel's board card (LED ring, cameras); without it the card says so.
Recommends: nexus-board
Description: Web panel for running commands on the Tridex Nexus PCB
 A web page on port 8080 for users who have never seen a terminal: log in
 with the admin account (sudo group), paste a command or press a button for
 a predefined one, and watch the output live. Buttons are defined in
 /etc/nexus/actions.json and run as root. Pasted commands run as the
 unprivileged user nexus-software (no sudo, no password). Unreachable from
 the open setup hotspot.
 .
 Also runs Autodarts (installed with the official installer, pasted into the
 panel) as nexus-software in the service nexus-autodarts, started and
 enabled from the panel. With nexus-board it switches the LED ring and
 shows which camera ports have a camera.

Package: nexus-web-terminal
Section: net
Architecture: arm64
Depends: login,
         ${misc:Depends}
Description: Browser terminal for the Tridex Nexus PCB
 ttyd on port 7681 running "login", so users sign in with a system account
 and get a shell in the browser. Unreachable from the open setup hotspot.
 .
 ttyd is not in Debian trixie; this package carries the pinned upstream
 static build in /usr/lib/nexus-web-terminal/.

Package: nexus-archive-keyring
Section: misc
Architecture: all
Multi-Arch: foreign
Depends: ${misc:Depends}
Description: Signing key and apt source of the Tridex Nexus repository
 Installs the OpenPGP key that signs the Nexus apt repository and the apt
 source pointing at it, so "apt upgrade" picks up new Nexus releases.
